--TEST--
A double whose shape of closures names a method its interface does not declare is refused — `rule:testing/doubles`, `rule:types/shape-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0079 § 10's second refusal, and the one that catches a renamed member:
// `tomorrow` stands in for nothing `Clock` declares, so the double would
// otherwise go on answering a contract no call site dispatches through — a
// test passing against a method that no longer exists.
//
// Width subtyping is what makes this a rule of its own rather than a
// consequence: `rule:types/shape-type` accepts a source with extra fields
// everywhere else, and a double is the one position where an extra field is a
// mistake, because the shape is being read as an implementation rather than as
// data.
interface Clock {
    public function now(): int;
}

Clock $clock = Core\Test::double<Clock>({
    now: fn(): int => 1700000000,
    tomorrow: fn(): int => 1700086400,
});
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0826]: `Clock` declares no method `tomorrow`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
